# Vercel Speed Insights Installation

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for this React + Vite project.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/speed-insights` version 2.0.0
- Updated package.json to include the new dependency
- Updated package-lock.json with the new package and its dependencies

### 2. Speed Insights Integration
- Added SpeedInsights import to src/App.tsx from `@vercel/speed-insights/react`
- Integrated the `<SpeedInsights />` component in the main App component
- Placed the component at the top level of the component tree, just after the BrowserRouter but within the QueryProvider context

### 3. Implementation Details
The SpeedInsights component was added following the official Vercel documentation for React applications. The component is positioned strategically to:
- Track page performance across all routes
- Work within the existing provider hierarchy
- Minimize impact on the application's rendering performance

### 4. Verification
- Build completed successfully without errors
- No new linting errors introduced (existing errors in the project remain)
- The package integrates seamlessly with the existing React Router setup
- No tests were available to run, but the build verification ensures the code compiles correctly

## Next Steps
After deploying to Vercel:
1. Enable Speed Insights in your Vercel dashboard
2. Navigate to Speed Insights from the project sidebar
3. Click "Enable" to activate the feature
4. Deploy the application to start collecting performance metrics
5. Review the Speed Insights dashboard to monitor Core Web Vitals and performance data

## Technical Notes
- This is a React application using Vite as the build tool
- The SpeedInsights component will automatically track Core Web Vitals
- Performance data will be sent to Vercel's analytics endpoints at `/_vercel/speed-insights/*` once enabled in the dashboard
- The component is lightweight and runs asynchronously to avoid blocking the main thread
